# Why I Love PINNs :)

The datasets I typically encounter in engineering fall into two categories:

### Case #1: Plenty of Data, Plenty of Noise

There are thousands of data points available, but the noise level is so high that even the testing engineer doubts the data.

### Case #2: Scarce Data, High Confidence

There are barely 10 data points available, but the testing engineer is confident about every single one of them. Of course, some noise is always part of engineering data, but the measurements themselves are considered trustworthy.

If you work in engineering domains long enough, you'll realize that this is the norm rather than the exception.

---

## Engineering's Blessing and Curse

Engineering domains come with a unique blessing.

Over decades, great scientists and engineers have built mathematical models describing how systems behave:

- Heat transfer equations
- Diffusion equations
- Conservation laws
- Electrochemistry equations
- Structural mechanics equations

In other words, we often know *how the system should behave*.

At the same time, engineering is filled with uncertainty:

- Manufacturing variations
- Material inconsistencies
- Measurement errors
- Process variations
- Test-to-test variations
- Environmental effects

This is the curse.

We have beautiful physics, but messy data.

